[method]
The paper used two-photon scanned light-sheet microscopy (2p-SPIM) to perform live imaging of zebrafish, including a beating heart, and to benchmark the modality against two-photon laser scanning microscopy (2p-LSM) and one-photon scanned light-sheet microscopy (1p-SPIM). The approach scanned a thin two-photon excitation light sheet through the sample and collected fluorescence through a dedicated detection path. It was used to characterize spatial resolution, signal rate at low average input power, imaging depth using a Fourier-space useful-contrast metric, and photodamage. This method is related to the proposed wide-field epifluorescence calcium imaging of 5 dpf zebrafish larvae because both are live larval zebrafish imaging methods, but it is not the same modality, does not use wide-field epifluorescence, and does not measure ΔF/F0 from manually selected whole-brain ROIs or test galanin manipulations.
[results]
The supplied excerpt does not provide numerical values, statistical tests, or significance levels. It reports that 2p-SPIM achieved a signal rate similar to 2p-LSM at low average input power, that imaging depth was quantified by analyzing spatial frequency components in Fourier space, that photodamage was low in fly experiments, and that 2p-SPIM enabled fast non-phototoxic imaging of a zebrafish beating heart. No quantitative resolution, frame rate, depth, input power, or ΔF/F0 values are available in the provided text.
[context]
The relevant experimental conditions involved live zebrafish and fly samples, with imaging parameters listed in the paper's supplementary materials but not reproduced in the supplied excerpt. The zebrafish demonstration imaged a beating heart rather than a whole brain, and the method used two-photon scanned light-sheet illumination rather than agarose-immobilized wide-field epifluorescence. For the proposed galanin whole-brain excitability plan, the paper is relevant mainly as evidence that low-phototoxic, high-speed live imaging of larval zebrafish is feasible, but it does not validate 1-hour calcium imaging, seizure models, or galanin receptor-specific effects.
[end related work 1]

