- in_text_citation: [(Zarei et al., 2021-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cbbb8b0e79344a142d14ffa7e62962ae82fbaf52)
- venue: BMC Biology
- citation count: 5
[method]
The paper used in vivo single-plane illumination microscopy (iSPIM) light-sheet calcium imaging of larval zebrafish to collect single-neuron activity across the forebrain and whole brain. Acquired images were preprocessed, drift-corrected, and segmented into neuronal regions of interest; ROIs were registered to a common anatomical template to enable cross-individual comparison. The analysis then classified ROI activity levels and ROI functional connectivity levels, used K-means clustering into five neuronal clusters, and generated noise-added, shuffled, and simulated surrogate networks using Cholesky decomposition to approximate ROI activity. This method is related to the proposed baseline larval zebrafish calcium activity analysis because it provides an imaging, preprocessing, ROI extraction, and activity-classification workflow for quantifying baseline neuronal activity, although the paper did not test galanin overexpression, gal knockout, or galr1a crispants and did not report event frequency, amplitude, or duration using 5% and 10% ΔF/F0 thresholds.
[results]
The method produced large-scale single-neuron activity maps and functional connectivity estimates in larval zebrafish. High neuronal activity and high functional connectivity occupied complementary domains in the forebrain, and neuronal populations with high activity versus high functional connectivity were largely non-overlapping in the whole brain. K-means clustering into five clusters yielded the same activity-versus-connectivity separation for both forebrain and whole brain data. Noise-added, shuffled, and simulated data produced activity-connectivity relationships that were distinct from the real brain data, indicating that the observed pattern was not an artifact of random or surrogate activity.
[context]
The experimental system was larval zebrafish imaged with iSPIM light-sheet microscopy at single-cell resolution, with data from multiple individual larvae registered to a common anatomical space for forebrain and whole-brain comparisons. The analysis emphasized spatial classification of activity and functional connectivity rather than pharmacological or genetic manipulation of galanin signaling. The reported method did not specify the exact calcium indicator line, imaging frame rate, exposure parameters, sample size, numerical activity thresholds, or statistical test values, so those reproducibility details cannot be reported from the supplied text. The method is relevant to the proposed plan's baseline wild-type and genetically manipulated larval zebrafish calcium imaging by demonstrating a scalable pipeline for ROI activity quantification and connectivity analysis, but it does not directly address seizure models, PTZ, eaat2a mutants, qPCR, or CRISPR generation of galr1a crispants.
[end related work 2]

