  "support": "Read as an iterative sequence, the strongest support comes from [(Jeong et al., 2019-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1ad3940ba2b748c00d0e9dd47d0c4cad77123847), which used qRT-PCR on adult zebrafish brain to quantify serotonin-related transcripts after spx1 overexpression in the dorsal habenula, demonstrating that qPCR can detect brain gene-expression changes associated with a defined neural phenotype in zebrafish. This directly supports the feasibility of the proposed qPCR validation of galanin transcripts in larval zebrafish brains, although it differs in developmental stage, target gene, and brain region. Next, [(Ahi et al., 2019-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/324fc9d52c65096c5f0450932db7a578dc05db91) supports the proposed qPCR workflow by describing RNA isolation, cDNA synthesis, candidate gene primer design, qPCR, and validation of suitable reference genes, which aligns with the plan to measure galanin transcript levels using reference genes. Older work by [(Cai et al., 2015-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6f76beacde776f1a3f08decca88f37cf90ace6df) provides weaker but relevant support because qRT-PCR was used to quantify cCART1 mRNA in chicken hypothalamus and pituitary after fasting, showing that qPCR is an established expression-validation assay for neuropeptide systems under physiological conditions, but it does not address zebrafish, larval brain, or galanin. Weakest support is from [(Botz et al., 2016-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1001bdf2d5155a63142dbdb5ef89e2a6f31a38b0), which included expression analysis of the galanin system in a GAL3 knockout arthritis model, indicating that galanin-related expression can be assessed in a genetic model, but the excerpt does not specify qPCR, tissue, primers, reference genes, or statistical details, so it provides only limited support for the proposed larval zebrafish brain qPCR design.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest contradiction is methodological: [(Ahi et al., 2019-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/324fc9d52c65096c5f0450932db7a578dc05db91) explicitly includes validation of suitable reference genes, whereas the proposed method assumes established reference genes are sufficient for larval zebrafish brains under genetic, seizure, and pharmacological conditions. Without condition-specific reference-gene validation, qPCR fold changes for galanin may be confounded by unstable normalization, especially across 5 dpf larvae, eaat2a-/- mutants, hsp70l:gal overexpression, gal-/-, galr1a crispants, and PTZ acute or rebound exposure. This newer validation emphasis also contrasts with the older, less detailed qPCR precedents in [(Cai et al., 2015-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6f76beacde776f1a3f08decca88f37cf90ace6df) and [(Jeong et al., 2019-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1ad3940ba2b748c00d0e9dd47d0c4cad77123847), which do not provide reference-gene validation details in the supplied excerpts. A second, more biological contradiction comes from [(Botz et al., 2016-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1001bdf2d5155a63142dbdb5ef89e2a6f31a38b0), which reported that galanin system expression profiles were not affected by GAL3 knockout in an arthritis model. Although the assay and tissue are unspecified, this finding cautions against assuming that receptor-level perturbations such as galr1a crispants will produce detectable changes in galanin transcript levels, and it weakens the expectation that qPCR of galanin alone will reveal receptor-specific mechanisms. Weaker contradictions arise from the lack of quantitative and protocol detail in the remaining works: [(Jeong et al., 2019-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1ad3940ba2b748c00d0e9dd47d0c4cad77123847) used adult zebrafish brain and serotonin-related genes rather than larval galanin, and [(Cai et al., 2015-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6f76beacde776f1a3f08decca88f37cf90ace6df) used chicken hypothalamus and pituitary for cCART1 after fasting; neither provides fold changes, p-values, sample sizes, primer sequences, reference genes, cycling conditions, or evidence that pools of 15 larvae are an appropriate biological replicate strategy for the proposed whole-brain qPCR.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important improvement is to validate reference genes in larval zebrafish brain under every planned condition, including wild-type, hsp70l:gal overexpression, gal-/-, galr1a crispants, eaat2a-/-, and PTZ acute or rebound exposure, using stability metrics such as geNorm, BestKeeper, or NormFinder and reporting Cq values, amplification efficiencies, and the final normalization strategy, as emphasized by [(Ahi et al., 2019-12)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/324fc9d52c65096c5f0450932db7a578dc05db91). The second priority is to clarify biological replication for the pools of 15 larvae: the plan should specify multiple independent pools per condition, for example 3 to 5 pools, so that Wilcoxon-Mann-Whitney tests or other qPCR statistics can be performed, and should include no-template, no-RT, standard-curve, and primer-specificity controls. Third, because [(Botz et al., 2016-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1001bdf2d5155a63142dbdb5ef89e2a6f31a38b0) found no galanin system expression change after GAL3 knockout, the method should not rely solely on galanin transcript levels to infer receptor-specific effects; it should also quantify galr1a and other galanin receptor transcripts, validate crispant genotypes by sequencing and expression, and use independent validation such as in situ hybridization, immunostaining, or Western blot for galanin peptide and receptor localization. Fourth, the qPCR design should be tightly aligned with the imaging plan by using 5 dpf whole larval brains from the same genotypes and PTZ protocol, including time-matched controls for 20 mM PTZ acute exposure and the 1-hour exposure plus 2-hour washout rebound protocol, and by reporting sample sizes, Cq values, fold changes, effect sizes, and p-values. Finally, to improve reproducibility relative to the limited detail in [(Jeong et al., 2019-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1ad3940ba2b748c00d0e9dd47d0c4cad77123847) and [(Cai et al., 2015-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6f76beacde776f1a3f08decca88f37cf90ace6df), the plan should specify primer sequences, RNA extraction and cDNA synthesis reagents, qPCR instrument, cycling conditions, pooling procedure, randomization, and blinding.",
